    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_162ea348",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: 1947_something_went_2017_terribly_right; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The meme juxtaposes a 1947 black-and-white portrait of a man in formal attire with a 2017 black-and-white photo of a Black woman walking across a street in a revealing outfit. The text '1947 something went...' and '2017... terribly right' implies a negative judgment on the 2017 image, suggesting that societal norms or standards have deteriorated. This comparison is inherently harmful as it uses a historical image to imply that contemporary expressions of Black femininity are 'wrong' or 'terrible,' which can reinforce negative stereotypes and contribute to the marginalization of Black women. The meme's intent is to provoke a reaction by framing modern Black female expression as a regression or failure, which can be interpreted as a form of cultural criticism that is potentially damaging to the self-esteem and social standing of Black women. The meme does not contain direct abusive or hateful language, but its underlying message can be seen as harmful due to its potential to perpetuate negative stereotypes and contribute to a hostile social environment.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ae6f839"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
